; The apex is hard and the edges are soft.
;
; This domain did the difficult part: -all on SPF and p=reject on DMARC. Then
; it left three doors open. sp=none exempts every subdomain, so a forger does
; not need the apex — mail from billing.gap.example reads as you to a person.
; pct=25 means three failures in four are delivered anyway. There is no rua,
; so nobody is watching any of it. And the DKIM key, generous as its size is,
; is still flagged t=y, which tells receivers to ignore the signature.
;
; Expected: a low grade on a policy that looks, at a glance, finished.

gap.example.            600 IN MX  10 mail.gap.example.

gap.example.            600 IN TXT "v=spf1 mx include:_spf.gap.example -all"

_dmarc.gap.example.     600 IN TXT "v=DMARC1; p=reject; sp=none; pct=25"

k1._domainkey.gap.example. 600 IN TXT "v=DKIM1; k=rsa; t=y; "

gap.example.            600 IN CAA 0 issue "sectigo.com"
